window.docContents[181].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___5___-Quotients/Quotient-algebra-is-well-defined_SEMI_-quotient-representations/#Chapter2___Discussion_2___5_well_defined":{"contents":"This is well defined because if \\pi(a) = \\pi(a'), then\n\n\\pi(a'b) = \\pi(ab + (a' - a)b) = \\pi(ab) + \\pi((a' - a)b) = \\pi(ab)\n\nbecause (a' - a)b \\in Ib \\subseteq I = \\ker \\pi, as I is a right ideal; similarly, if \\pi(b) = \\pi(b'), then\n\n\\pi(ab') = \\pi(ab + a(b' - b)) = \\pi(ab) + \\pi(a(b' - b)) = \\pi(ab)\n\nbecause a(b' - b) \\in aI \\subseteq I = \\ker \\pi, as I is also a left ideal. Thus, A/I is an algebra.\n\nSimilarly, if V is a representation of A and W \\subset V is a subrepresentation, then V/W is also a representation. Indeed, let \\pi : V \\to V/W be the quotient map, and set \\rho_{V/W}(a)\\pi(x) := \\pi(\\rho_V(a)x).\n\nAbove we noted that left ideals of A are subrepresentations of the regular representation of A, and vice versa. Thus, if I is a left ideal in A, then A/I is a representation of A.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.5. Quotients\u0009Quotient algebra is well-defined; quotient representations","header":"Quotient algebra is well-defined; quotient representations","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___5___-Quotients/Quotient-algebra-is-well-defined_SEMI_-quotient-representations/#Chapter2___Discussion_2___5_well_defined"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___7___-Examples-of-algebras/Representations-and-ideals-of-the-Weyl-algebra/#Chapter2___Problem2___7___4___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.7. Examples of algebras\u0009Representations and ideals of the Weyl algebra\u0009Representations and ideals of the Weyl algebra","header":"Formalization","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___7___-Examples-of-algebras/Representations-and-ideals-of-the-Weyl-algebra/#Chapter2___Problem2___7___4___formalization"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___8___-Quivers/Subrepresentation-of-a-quiver-representation/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___8___-Quivers--Subrepresentation-of-a-quiver-representation--Subrepresentation-of-a-quiver-representation--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"An auxiliary type associated with a commutative semiring and a quiver.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Definition2.8.8; role=supporting]Returns the submodule associated with a vertex of the auxiliary type.An arrow map sends an element of the source submodule into the target submodule associated with the auxiliary type.\n\nConverts the auxiliary type into its associated quiver-indexed object.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Definition2.8.8; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.8. Quivers\u0009Subrepresentation of a quiver representation\u0009Subrepresentation of a quiver representation\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___8___-Quivers/Subrepresentation-of-a-quiver-representation/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___8___-Quivers--Subrepresentation-of-a-quiver-representation--Subrepresentation-of-a-quiver-representation--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___6___-Characters-of-representations/Section-3___6___-Characters-of-representations-___-heading-and-character-definition/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___6___-Characters-of-representations--Section-3___6___-Characters-of-representations-___-heading-and-character-definition":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.6. Characters of representations","header":"Section 3.6: Characters of representations — heading and character definition","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___6___-Characters-of-representations/Section-3___6___-Characters-of-representations-___-heading-and-character-definition/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___6___-Characters-of-representations--Section-3___6___-Characters-of-representations-___-heading-and-character-definition"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___21___-Schur-polynomials/Definition-of-D_lambda-and-Schur-polynomials-S_lambda/#Chapter5___Discussion_Schur_polynomials___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.21. Schur polynomials\u0009Definition of D_lambda and Schur polynomials S_lambda\u0009Definition of D_lambda and Schur polynomials S_lambda","header":"Formalization","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___21___-Schur-polynomials/Definition-of-D_lambda-and-Schur-polynomials-S_lambda/#Chapter5___Discussion_Schur_polynomials___formalization"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___25___-Representations-of-___GL_2_LPAR____mathbb___F____q_RPAR____/5___25___2___-1-dimensional-representations/#Chapter5___Proposition5___25___1":{"contents":"Proposition 5.25.1. [G, G] = SL_2(\\mathbb{F}_q).\n\nProof. Clearly,\n\n\\det(xyx^{-1}y^{-1}) = 1,\n\nso\n\n[G, G] \\subseteq SL_2(\\mathbb{F}_q).\n\nTo show the converse, it suffices to show that the matrices\n\n\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}, \\qquad \\begin{pmatrix} a & 0 \\\\ 0 & a^{-1} \\end{pmatrix}, \\qquad \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\nare commutators (as such matrices generate SL_2(\\mathbb{F}_q)). Clearly, by using transposition, it suffices to show that only the first two matrices are commutators. But it is easy to see that the matrix\n\n\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}\nis the commutator of the matrices\n\nA = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1/2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}, \\qquad B = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\end{pmatrix},\n\nwhile the matrix\n\n\\begin{pmatrix} a & 0 \\\\ 0 & a^{-1} \\end{pmatrix}\n\nis the commutator of the matrices\n\nA = \\begin{pmatrix} a & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}, \\qquad B = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\nThis completes the proof. \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.25. Representations of $GL_2(\\mathbb{F}_q)$\u00095.25.2. 1-dimensional representations\u0009[G,G] = SL_2(F_q)","header":"[G,G] = SL_2(F_q)","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___25___-Representations-of-___GL_2_LPAR____mathbb___F____q_RPAR____/5___25___2___-1-dimensional-representations/#Chapter5___Proposition5___25___1"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___9___-Exact-functors/Additive-functors-on-semisimple-categories-are-exact/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--7___-Introduction-to-categories--7___9___-Exact-functors--Additive-functors-on-semisimple-categories-are-exact":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.9. Exact functors","header":"Additive functors on semisimple categories are exact","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___9___-Exact-functors/Additive-functors-on-semisimple-categories-are-exact/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--7___-Introduction-to-categories--7___9___-Exact-functors--Additive-functors-on-semisimple-categories-are-exact"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___6___-Finite-abelian-categories/Characterization-of-projective-generators-in-finite-abelian-categories/#Chapter9___Exercise9___6___3___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.6. Finite abelian categories\u0009Characterization of projective generators in finite abelian categories\u0009Characterization of projective generators in finite abelian categories","header":"Formalization","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___6___-Finite-abelian-categories/Characterization-of-projective-generators-in-finite-abelian-categories/#Chapter9___Exercise9___6___3___formalization"}});